            note: "〜ば focuses on the condition itself: if (and only if) A, then B. It's typical for advice, hypotheticals and \"what it takes\": 安ければ買います, 急げば間に合う. 〜ばよかった is the standard way to say \"I should have\": もっと勉強すればよかった. When A is an action, the second half usually can't be a request, invitation or intention (着けば電話してください sounds off; use たら), though it's fine when A is a state: 時間があれば来てください. Watch いい → よければ (never いければ), and in casual speech なければ often shrinks to なきゃ.",
